# Your first document

@lede
Write a `.brf` file. Compile it. Read the output. That is the loop.
@end

## A minimum file

Save the following as `notes.brf`:

```brief
# Quarterly notes

The team delivered *strong* numbers, with _revenue_ up 23 percent.

- shipped the v0.4 release
- closed the old auth refactor
- backlog: error message rewrite

@callout(kind: tip)
Press @kbd[Ctrl+C] to abort the long-running build.
@end
```

That is a complete, valid Brief document. Five constructs are at play —
heading, paragraph with emphasis, unordered list, block callout, inline
keyboard input.

## Compile it

```bash
brief compile notes.brf --target=html
```

By default the rendering goes to `stdout`. Pipe it to a file or pass `-w`
to write next to the source:

```bash
brief compile notes.brf --target=html -w           # writes notes.html
brief compile notes.brf --target=html -o out.html  # explicit path
```

The same source compiles for an LLM:

```bash
brief compile notes.brf --target=llm -w            # writes notes.txt
brief compile notes.brf --target=llm --report-tokens
```

`--report-tokens` adds a stderr line with the cl100k character/token
estimate, useful for budgeting LLM prompts.

## What the compiler enforces

Try changing `*strong*` to `**strong**`. Recompile:

@diagnostic
```text
error[B0206]: doubled emphasis markers are not valid; use a single marker
  --> notes.brf:3:21
   |
 3 | The team delivered **strong** numbers, with _revenue_ up 23 percent.
   |                    ^^ use a single `*` on each side
   |
   = help: Brief uses one marker per side. Doubled markers are reserved
           for diagnostic clarity, not as a second emphasis style.
```
@end

This is the entire ethos. Ambiguous input is a compile error, with a span
and a code. Run `brief explain B0206` for the long form.

## A first project

A project is a directory with a `brief.toml` at its root. Most useful
features — custom shortcodes, cross-document `@ref` links, the formatter's
defaults — read configuration from there.

```bash
mkdir my-project
cd my-project
cat > brief.toml <<'EOF'
[project]
name = "my-project"

[compile]
default_target = "html"
EOF
echo '# Hello' > index.brf
brief compile index.brf --target=html -w
```

You now have a project. Adding more `.brf` files, or registering custom
shortcodes in `brief.toml`, is incremental from here.

